{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection; rg -n 'chooses a new thesis|conclusions it relies|dependencies' docs/prd/06* | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/codex-r1.stream\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/brief.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/claude-r1.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/codex-r1.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/round2-brief.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/codex-r2.stream\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/codex-r2.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/RESULT.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/EDGE-PROOF-RESULT.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/claude-reader.jsonl\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/separation_proof_2.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/packet.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/separation_proof.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/seam_plugin.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/brief.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/edge_proof.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/edge_inventory.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/separation-proof.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/tally_pairs.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/separation-proof-2.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/filing-contract.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/old-rows-readers.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/codex-reader.jsonl\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/build_pairs.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/edge-proof.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/separated_edges.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/old_rows_readers.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/DEPENDENCY-SELECTION-CENSUS.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/own-read-before-readers.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/filing_contract.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/tally.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/edge-inventory.json\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/pairs.json\n20:- A theme's outcome can help one asset and harm another. Theme ladders describe outcomes; asset dependencies specify their own direction.\n26:- The model chooses dependencies like an analyst; code rejects cycles. Apparently mutual dependencies indicate poorly defined questions.\n78:3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.\n92:- Code checks cited IDs against the request that was sent: evidence must be among the pieces shown to this thesis (D198); cited theses must be its dependencies. Accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise record the reason and ask for one corrected judgement. If that also fails, use normal failure handling: retry from this step, then human review after repeated failure. This checks references, not whether the judgement is correct, like verifying a cited page exists.\n116:<!-- generated: dependencies -->\n134:- The matching call chooses a new thesis's dependencies, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction (D51). Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Jev, the default matcher, is not asked for them, so new theses do not receive dependencies through the default matcher; building the choice on the Jev path is decided (D368). The historical seed questions are retired (D370). A historical seed's dependency arrow means \"supports\" (D368, by D208): for filing, the supporting (driver) thesis is the narrower one; for judging, the supported (root) thesis reads the supporting theses' conclusions, so drivers and themes are judged before their roots. The judging code reads the stored edge the other way today; the fix is to be built and measured (D368). Completed events are evidence, not dependencies: today's oil price influencing OPEC output is evidence for the OPEC-output thesis.\n136:<!-- /generated: dependencies -->\n168:  ↓ 4 Match (Model): The matcher does not read the original item. Jev answers, with a score, each candidate's relation to every thesis on its list (shown with its subject, question, focus, ladder, due date and open/closed state) and each piece of evidence's relation to every stored piece on its list under each linked thesis; code turns the answers into the destinations below and owns identity on typed keys (D155, D169, D197). When a candidate's list holds a thesis of its own subject and measure, the collision judge, one generative call, answers the analyst's question for those theses under D248's test, and its verdict replaces Jev's (D190, D258). Jev is not asked for dependencies. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, then searches all linked existing theses together and is compared once more. Evidence linked only to new theses is new evidence.\n171:  ↓ 7 Run the day's rejudgement round (Model): Once a day, after the day's last item (D63), rejudge only open theses whose inputs changed that day: new or newly linked evidence (a repeat or a revision is a new piece), or a changed dependency conclusion, newly created awaited thesis or successor. Judge dependencies first, then theme theses before asset theses, and each thesis once per round; each judgement reads the evidence in view (D198). Extra source references alone do not trigger it. Memo-derived theses without evidence stay not yet judged. Each judgement declares whether its conclusion changed; changed conclusions propagate to dependants, and a theme's to every open asset thesis (D195). The round's queue is read from the ledger, so a resumed round recovers what an earlier commit queued.\n240:Jev judges step 4, and the collision judge, a generative model, answers for a candidate whose list holds a thesis of its own subject and measure (D169, D190, D258). Retain every decision and have people label code-refused cases and periodic random samples: candidate destinations, dependencies and evidence decisions. When sufficient labels exist, train a faster smaller model to take over. Details remain deferred.\n263:| Decision | Each candidate's destination and reason; new-thesis dependencies and directions; evidence repeat/replacement/new decisions; validation results and refusal reasons |\n318:- One queue writes each thesis: the theme's queue for theme theses; the company, cryptocurrency or commodity queue for asset theses. Within an item, write themes before assets. Rejudgement order follows dependencies under the round rule. Other queues send requests, such as dependency changes or replacement links, to the owning queue with the item's sequence number.\n320:- A day's items form one round: each item's evidence links, thesis creation, succession and dependencies are written in sequence order, and after the day's last item the round rejudges in dependency order (D63). Dependants read conclusions freshly judged in that round. Judge each thesis once. A widely depended-on thesis such as the Fed rate triggers each dependant once only if it declares its conclusion changed; otherwise propagation stops.\n346:- Every comparison records shown input, raw response, Config version and decision maker. Reject destinations outside the shown lists or relevant evidence, and dependencies not shown or explicitly awaiting a chain/year, including cycles.\n350:- Choose dependencies during matching from shown theses or explicitly awaited chain/years (D51; decided, not built on the default Jev path, which asks for none). Numeric dependencies read the specified year; event dependencies follow succession. No cycles.\n351:- Judge dependencies before dependants and each thesis once per day's round (D63).\n368:1. Proposal, not decided (D356): daily pairwise duplicate checks among open theses of a subject; merge the later thesis into the earlier, move links and dependencies, check cycles and rejudge. Add separate duplicate-check settings to Config.\n376:- D38–D40: question/ladder/due-date structure, state, evidence links/tiers, dependencies/succession, round-based intake, history and matching labels.\n377:- D41/D42: align frozen modules with the rebuilt library, separate evidence/candidates, compare within-thesis evidence, and permit binary ladders; the extraction request they described (with dependencies) became open theses with their takes lines (D211, D264). D375 supersedes D251's day-start timing with the latest committed questions before each item.\n398:- Matching still decides thesis/evidence identity and dependencies; it does not reread association fields to regroup. After creation, succession or merging, the writer resolves candidate IDs to final thesis IDs and local group IDs to stored group IDs, then stores membership. Candidates failing source checks or ultimately skipped do not become members. Do not create empty groups for unused declarations or reject other valid memberships because of them.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/DEPENDENCY-SELECTION-CENSUS.md; rg -n '\"'^#|reads.*supports|historical|theme shown|cycle'\"' docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md | head -58\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/DEPENDENCY-SELECTION-CENSUS.md; rg -n '\"'^#|reads.*supports|historical|theme shown|cycle'\"' docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md | head -58\"","aggregated_output":"# Where could a new question have been given a dependency? A design census on the unseeded fresh week (D536, under D439), 2026-11-15, $0\n\nScripts and files beside this page: `build_pairs.py` (`pairs.json`, `packet.md`), `brief.md`, `own-read-before-readers.json` (Claude's\nread, written before any reader), `codex-reader.jsonl` and `claude-reader.jsonl` (two blind readers of different model families, each\ngiven only the packet and the brief in a clean folder), `tally_pairs.py` (`tally.json`). The run: the old system's stored draw of the\nfresh week (`poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week/p1/prod`: 235 articles, 15 questions, no dependency written). Nothing was sent\nto a model and nothing changed in either system. This is a census for a design, not a claim about what the matching step would\nchoose, and not a test of any answer.\n\n## The answer\n\n**One clear case in the week, and a negative control; the census is not stopped.** When the data-center revenue question opened, the\nsystem had just compared it with the total-revenue question and recorded \"part of\" (1.00). All three readings mark that pair as\nstrong. The total-revenue question's conclusion stated a consensus for the year from 2026-09-23 ($406.05 billion; about $412\nbillion from 09-25) and the next quarter's guidance of $108 billion from 09-26; none of these three figures was anywhere in the\ndata-center question's seven requests, and its last answer says so and closes the gap with a step of its own. That is the one answer of this system's fifteen that both reviewing teams of the week marked as\nresting on a number nobody gave it. A dependency chosen when the question opened is the route PRD 06 names for this (\"A statement\nabout A is A's evidence ... B learns it through A's conclusion\"). Whether the judge would have used the line is not tested, and\nthe line would not by itself make the answer supported: the total's conclusion holds forecasts and assumptions of its own, and\nthe data-center judge would still have to turn company-wide figures into a segment's (council round 28).\n\nThe relations an analyst would most want run the other way in time: the Rubin shipping questions opened after the revenue\nquestions they bear on, and a dependency is chosen only when a question is created.\n\n## The pairs\n\nThe rule in force (PRD 06, D51): the matching call chooses a new question's dependencies only among the questions it was compared\nwith, the questions shown that day, and the candidates opened from the same article; \"B depends on A\" means B reads A's conclusion.\nOver the 15 openings that gives **97 pairs** (95 compared when the question opened, 2 from one article). Each reader gave every\npair one mark: the new question would want the other's conclusion; only the other way; both; the same question (or another period\nor deadline of it); none. The two blind readers give the same mark on 68 of the 97.\n\n| | Both blind readers | Claude's own read (written first) |\n|---|---|---|\n| the new question would want the earlier question's conclusion | 11 (3 marked strong by both) | 5 (3 strong) |\n| ... of which the other question is a theme already shown to it | 0 (one reader: 1) | 0 |\n| only the other way, or both ways | 10 | 15 |\n| ... of which the new question is a company question, so the earlier question could only gain the link later | 8 | 9 |\n| both ways (a second link would close a circle) | 3 | 1 |\n| the same question, or another period of it | 1 pair, marked in both of its orders (two of three readings: 3 pairs) | 3 pairs |\n| none | 57 by one reader, 69 by the other | 75 |\n\nWhere the readings part (all kept in `tally_pairs.py`'s output): one blind reader marked no pair between a new theme and an earlier\ncompany question (the packet says themes are shown to every company question already), the other marked sixteen; total revenue of\none year against the next is \"the same thing, another period\" for both blind readers and an input in Claude's read; next year's\nmarket spending against this year's is an input for both blind readers and a successor in Claude's read and in the run's own\nrecord. Where \"an analyst reasons onward\" stops is not settled by these readers, as in the earlier censuses.\n\n## The eleven pairs both blind readers marked, read against the stored conclusions\n\nFor each: what the earlier question's conclusion would have shown (its first 600 characters, as a request shows a dependency) at\nthe new question's judgements, and whether the new question's whole request already held it.\n\n| New question ← earlier question | Readers | What the line would carry | Read |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| data-center revenue FY2027 ← total revenue FY2027 | strong, all three (both ways) | the $406.05 billion consensus from 09-23, about $412 billion from 09-25, the $108 billion guidance from 09-26: none of the three anywhere in the new question's seven requests (the $96.2 billion quarter the line also names was already in all seven) | **useful and source-backed** (six cited passages); the question's last answer: \"no full-FY2027 Data Center consensus or annual company target\", then \"a further roughly 25% step-up in Q4\" of its own |\n| total revenue growth FY2028 ← total revenue FY2027 | strong, all three | the same figures | arguable: the question asks a growth rate and holds the growth consensus and guidance; the base adds little. The question is itself a second form of the FY2028 revenue question |\n| market AI spending 2027 ← cloud companies' spending 2026 (both themes) | strong, both blind | \"nearly $800 billion for calendar 2026\" | nothing new: the new question's answer already uses those figures; the run recorded the pair as one quantity's next year |\n| gross margin FY2028 ← Rubin shipping by January 2027 | one strong, one weak | production shipments began in August 2026 | second-order: the answer rests on management's margin guidance |\n| gross margin FY2028 ← total revenue FY2028; ← its growth | weak, both | revenue figures | context only |\n| the two questions on China's permission for one card ← U.S. authorization of Blackwell exports (all themes) | weak, both (both ways) | the standing U.S. restriction, no figure | context only; a theme is not shown other themes, so a link would be the only route |\n| total revenue growth FY2028 ← data-center revenue FY2027 | weak, both | **the data-center judge's own numbers** ($105 billion, $131 billion: the step nobody gave it) | a risk, not a gain: a dependency would pass an assumed number on |\n| total revenue FY2028 and its growth ← financing commitments | weak, both | guarantee ceilings of $105 and $108.5 billion | context only |\n\n**Negative controls.** The question on the Hugging Face purchase commitments against its eleven earlier questions, the financing-\ncommitments question among them (both are amounts in the same note of the annual report): \"none\" in all three readings, and the\nfinancing conclusion carries nothing it needs. And the next-to-last row above: a link two readers would grant weakly that would\ncarry an unsupported number.\n\n## The other direction, and what already covers part of it\n\n- **A later question that an earlier one would want** (both blind readers, new company question): Rubin shipping by January 2027\n  against the four revenue questions; Rubin GPUs by December 2027 against the two FY2028 revenue questions; data-center revenue\n  against the two total-revenue questions. Read in the store: over all their requests, three of the four revenue questions held\n  no statement naming Rubin (the data-center question held ten). A dependency chosen at creation cannot serve these; D191 point 2\n  (a later article may add a dependency to an existing question) is decided and unbuilt.\n- **A later theme that earlier company questions would want** (export authorization, the cloud companies' spending, the Chinese\n  permissions): the theme block already shows every judged theme to every company question; no link is needed (D191).\n- **Circles.** Data-center revenue and total revenue are marked both ways by all three readings: the part is sized from the\n  whole, and the whole is mostly the part. Only one of the two links can exist (PRD 06 refuses a cycle); at creation the\n  permitted one is the new question reading the earlier one, here the part reading the whole.\n\n## What the matching step already records\n\nEvery new question's candidate is compared with each listed question and one relation of a closed set is stored (`RELATIONS` in\n`views/jev_matching.py`: the same quantity or event; the same quantity for another period; part of; one matter, two quantities;\ndifferent; cannot tell). In this week: 85 \"different\", 3 \"part of\", 2 \"another period\", 4 \"one matter, two quantities\", 3 without a\nrelation. The one useful pair is the one recorded \"part of\" at 1.00. The relation is not the choice: of the nine pairs recorded\nas other than \"different\", both blind readers want the conclusion on five; of the 85 \"different\", on five (all but one weak).\nWhether two questions are part and whole is identity; whether one needs the other's conclusion is the model's judgement (AGENTS §2).\n\n## What this may and may not be said to show\n\n- Shown, for one stored week and one draw: the pairs on which a dependency could have been chosen; one pair where the earlier\n  question's conclusion held source-backed figures the new question never received and whose absence its last answer names; that\n  most pairs readers grant carry nothing the question needs; one pair where a link would carry an assumed number; that the\n  strongest driver relations of the week are not available at creation.\n- Not shown: that the judge would use the line, or that the answer would become supported (no send); a rate (fifteen questions,\n  one clear case); what the matching step would choose if asked; anything about seeded edges or the reversal of D368 item 4 (the\n  useful pair here is a part reading its whole, the stored direction of the seeded set); anything about the new system, which has\n  no such mechanism; which system carries forward.\n- The same figures could also have reached the question by filing (the sixteen guidance statements sit under total revenue only;\n  D207 gives a statement one home, and PRD 06 gives the other question the conclusion instead). This census does not choose\n  between the two routes.\n1:# Design for the council's review: the two uses of a dependency edge, read apart (D538, under D439), 2026-11-15, $0\n12:## 1. The defect, in one picture\n18:| judging | `store.dependencies_of`, `store.dependants_of`, `store.would_cycle` (the request's block, the order of a round, the citations allowed, what a changed conclusion queues, the cycle check) | \"this question reads that question's conclusion\" |\n28:## 2. The rule\n32:- **Reading relation** (judging): for a `reads` edge (B, A), B reads A's conclusion. For a `supports` edge (D, R), R reads D's.\n40:### 2.1 What the rule changes in a decision's reading, and what it costs\n55:### 2.2 Duplicates and circles, on the reading relation\n64:| R reads D (from `supports` (D, R)) | D reads R (`reads` (D, R)) | refused as a circle |\n65:| R reads D (from `supports` (D, R)) | R reads D again (`reads` (R, D), any label) | **not resolvable here: returned to the council.** The seed edge's label is `last_end`, written as a constant by the seeding module; no decision, design page, seed file or prompt defines it, so \"the same direction\" and \"a conflicting one\" cannot be told apart without inventing its meaning. The proof writes nothing and reports \"label undefined\" |\n66:| a longer path from the read question back to the reader, through edges of either kind | any edge that closes it | refused as a circle (PRD 06: \"Before adding an edge ... code checks for cycles\") |\n71:### 2.3 Rows written before a kind was stored\n84:### 2.4 The one-home requirement: what is represented, and what is left unresolved\n92:### 2.5 The filing contract, case by case (`filing_contract.py`, `filing-contract.json`)\n98:| Case | The reading step linked | No stated hierarchy (today for article-opened questions; a `reads` edge) | A stated hierarchy (a `supports` edge; D265 read literally on a `reads` edge) | Expected, and by which words |\n120:So the contract this design can state for filing is narrow: **a `reads` edge moves no statement; a `supports` edge keeps D265's\n124:### 2.6 The unseeded path: what can happen to a dependency chosen at creation, and who already handles it (round 32)\n134:| a circle among the candidates of one answer; conflicting directions between same-key candidates | yes | PRD 06: \"Refuse a cycle and ask the model to correct it; persistent cycles follow normal failure handling\"; \"A dependency has one direction\" | `matching.check` and `identity.batch_plan` refuse; the refusal is in the match record | unchanged | the suite under the seam (`test_coalesced_dependency_is_refused_before_matching_retry_ends`) |\n135:| a circle against edges already stored | **yes, in one way** (corrected after round 33): nothing reads a question that is being created, except an existing question that was waiting for it. If the new question names that waiting question as its dependency, binding the waiting edge would close a circle; `resolve_waiting` checks and leaves the edge unbound, without a record | PRD 06, as above | `writing.write` drops the edge with a note in the article's record. **This differs from PRD 06 (refuse and ask for a correction)**; the difference predates this design and can only matter for a later addition | the cycle check walks the reading relation | proofs 1 and 2 (the check itself) |\n137:| the read question closes, gains a successor, or its awaited year is created | yes | PRD 06's tree (read the closing conclusion; read the successor and judge once; wait and bind) | the request marks \"closed\" or \"historical only\"; resolution rows; `resolve_waiting` with the cycle check | unchanged: `reads` rows pass through the same functions | the suite under the seam (the awaited year; resolutions in the bundle and the research context). No test covers \"historical only\", and no code in the package writes that resolution (the request can print the mark; nothing sets it): PRD 06's branch for a question closed without a successor has a reader and no writer |\n150:through the service at one far date, not at every historical day. What the candidate leaves undetermined: the later addition\n151:(its conflict and its cycle handling), and everything that touches a seed edge (the label, 2.2; the seeded pages, where the\n154:## 3. Table 1: state of a pair by event\n158:| Event | No edge | `reads` edge (B, A) | `supports` edge (D, R) |\n160:| a question B is created and the matching call names A (D51) | cycle check on the reading relation, then the edge is written with kind `reads` at the article's seq, in the same commit as B (`writing.write` and the \"written\" record are one commit); B's first judgement that evening reads A's conclusion | the writer's own duplicate check writes nothing twice (a resumed article) | a new B has no edge yet: not reachable |\n161:| a later article names a dependency for an existing question (D191 point 2; decided, unbuilt) | cycle check, then a `reads` edge at that article's seq; the question is judged once in that day's round (its inputs changed, PRD 06) | nothing written | the same pair the other way round: a circle, refused (next row) |\n162:| the proposed edge would close a circle in the reading relation | refused: nothing written, a note in the article's record (\"dropped: cycle\", as `writing.py` does today); the question opens without it | as left | as left |\n164:| the read question closes | nothing | with a successor: a resolution row to the successor, B judged once; without: B reads the closing conclusion marked \"historical only\" (PRD 06, as today) | R reads D's closing conclusion as \"historical only\"; seed edges have no successor rule. Unexercised: no stored draw closes a question |\n165:| the awaited year's question is created (a waiting `reads` edge) | nothing | bound by `resolve_waiting` after the cycle check; B judged once (as today) | a seed edge carries no awaited year: not reachable |\n169:| a row written before a kind was stored (2.3) | nothing | a row at an article's seq, on a question opened from an article, with a matching direction label: `reads`, as judging has always read it | a row with the seeding module's marks: `supports`. A row with neither writer's marks: refused |\n174:## 4. Table 2: each value by its readers\n184:| `store.would_cycle` (`writing.write`, `resolve_waiting`) | walks the stored rows | walks the reading relation, across both kinds |\n195:## 5. Table 3: the new reading against the checks the old one passes\n202:| a cycle is refused | `store.would_cycle` | refused across both kinds (section 8, C) |\n211:## 6. Table 4: prior decisions\n216:| PRD 06, Dependencies | \"B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion\"; \"A statement about A is A's evidence\"; \"Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item\"; cycles refused | the reading relation; no hierarchy from a `reads` edge |\n226:| The independent review of 2026-11-03 (`review-core-codex.md`, point 6) | six contracts a fix must settle: filing's use, cycles across kinds, article-opened questions' edges keeping their meaning, the direction label, citations, the 600-character cut | the first, second, third and fifth are answered by the rule and section 8; the fourth and sixth are open (section 7) |\n228:## 7. What this design leaves open (for the council; nothing here is assumed)\n254:    refuse and ask for a correction; an awaited edge that would close a circle is left unbound without a record; \"historical\n260:## 8. The first proof on stored data (`separation_proof.py`, `separated_edges.py`), $0\n270:  unrelated pair included; the judge's check accepts the citation; the reverse edge is refused as a cycle.\n285:## 9. The second proof (`separation_proof_2.py`), $0: the production writers, a whole week, the edge combinations, old rows\n303:- **X. The combinations of 2.2** on the seeded state: `reads` (total revenue, data-center revenue) on top of the `supports` edge\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
