{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'ls -lah /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/dep-probe{,-check}; cat docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/dep-probe:\ntotal 112392\ndrwxr-xr-x@   5 lchingen  wheel   160B Nov 20 17:19 .\ndrwx------@ 324 lchingen  wheel    10K Nov 20 17:21 ..\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    19M Nov 20 17:19 old-1-views.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    18M Nov 20 17:19 old-2-views.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    18M Nov 20 17:19 old-3-views.sqlite\n\n/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/dep-probe-check:\ntotal 224784\ndrwxr-xr-x@   8 lchingen  wheel   256B Nov 20 17:21 .\ndrwx------@ 324 lchingen  wheel    10K Nov 20 17:21 ..\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    19M Nov 20 17:21 old-1-changed.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    19M Nov 20 17:21 old-1-control.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    18M Nov 20 17:21 old-2-changed.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    18M Nov 20 17:21 old-2-control.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    18M Nov 20 17:21 old-3-changed.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    18M Nov 20 17:21 old-3-control.sqlite\n# Council Q: would an analyst use poc-v118's library? (2026-11-20; consensus in round 2; $0)\n\nAlex, asked to read the served 09-20 library himself: \"dont' you take a look for me let the council tak ea crack at it\". Record:\n`brief.md`, `library-pack.md` (the served library as text), Codex round 1 `codex-r1.md` (thread `01a1fba6-8066-7031-9a26-da22b7dcbd3d`,\nread-only, memories off), Claude's read written before reading Codex's `claude-r1.md`, round 2 `round2-brief.md` and `codex-r2.md`.\nBoth readers are models; no person has read the output yet.\n\n## Answer\n**PARTLY.** An analyst would use it to find what happened, who said it and the source, and to check milestones. They would not use\nits number answers, because linked number questions contradict each other.\n\n## What both readers found\n- **Most useful:** the evidence is faithful. Every claim checked against its article was right (Claude 10, Codex 10; one Codex \"wrong\"\n  is the $12.9B Hugging Face total, which the 8-K splits as $11.9B plus up to $1.0B retention). Settled events are right on primary\n  sources: v4 (Vera Rubin production shipments began in August, earnings call and 10-Q), v34 (8-K: agreement signed 2026-09-02), v3.\n- **Most unsatisfying:** v9 (FY2027 Data Center revenue) carries Q4 Data Center at about $146B while v14 (FY2027 total revenue)\n  carries all of Q4 at about $127B. v9's \"market reference\" of $414B is its own estimate of 09-12/13 relabelled from 09-15, and on\n  09-20 it moved a band on a SemiAnalysis forecast dated 07-26. Also v8 against v16 (CPU), v35 against v14 (FY2028 base), and v21\n  applying the FY2028 70% outlook to FY2027.\n- **It recurs in all three draws** (read at $0 through `bundle.library` at 09-20): old-1 Data Center Q4 about $126B against a total\n  Q4 of about $112B; old-3 a total of about $446B against Data Center of about $375B (an 84% mix, weaker evidence).\n- **Mechanism:** no question has a dependency and no judgement cites another question (0 of 139 questions, 0 of 4,932 judgements\n  across the three draws). Each number question is judged alone. The missing piece is decided and not built: D368 item 2 (Alex,\n  2026-11-05: \"2. 做\"), D51 and D191 point 2; D368 item 4 sets the direction: the broader (root) question reads its narrower\n  drivers' conclusions.\n- **Also:** duplicates (v1/v2, v3/v22, v30/v31, v14/v21, v17/v35, v8/v16); small or non-NVIDIA bets (v11, v13, v19, v25, v26, v32,\n  v36, v38, v41, v42); missing bets (the next quarter, Blackwell volumes through the Rubin transition, AMD and customers' own chips,\n  China revenue); v6 overrides the $1.08T consensus with NVIDIA's own top-five $1.3T scaled to four; v5's figures are total capex\n  for a data-center-capex question; v15's band rests on an unsupported $28B; v43's Yes rests on the target close date; 4 of 25\n  sampled uncited evidence items worth keeping (Codex, one sample); the page opens on the newest questions.\n\n## Recommendation (consensus) and one test\nDo not build the daily wrapper yet: a daily refresh would deliver the same contradictions every day. First close the cross-question\ncontradiction with the decided dependencies. It changes frozen matching and judging, so it is a new version Alex opens.\n1. **$0 first:** render the exact judging request in D368's direction (the total-revenue question reading the Data Center\n   question's conclusion, `views/judging.py` `_lines_english`) and check that the decisive figures (Q4, the reference) survive the\n   600-character cut.\n2. **Then a few cents, only if they do:** re-judge the three draws' linked revenue questions at 09-20 with the dependency, three\n   samples each beside fresh unchanged controls. Pass: source-backed quarterly figures that reconcile (Data Center never above total).\n   Misleading if a forced mix or a later answer fed back manufactures agreement.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '28,55p;224,259p' docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md; rg -n 'budget|def call|def build_request|retire' src/hivemind/model.py; rg -n 'prompt or acceptance|The ruler|7.1' AGENTS.md | head -12\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '28,55p;224,259p' docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md; rg -n 'budget|def call|def build_request|retire' src/hivemind/model.py; rg -n 'prompt or acceptance|The ruler|7.1' AGENTS.md | head -12\"","aggregated_output":"## 2. The rule\n\nEach edge carries its kind, set by its writer and by nothing else: `supports` or `reads`. Each reader reads one derived relation.\n\n- **Reading relation** (judging): for a `reads` edge (B, A), B reads A's conclusion. For a `supports` edge (D, R), R reads D's.\n- **Narrower relation** (filing): for a `supports` edge (D, R), D is the narrower home than R. A `reads` edge states none.\n\nSources: D368 item 4 (\"For filing, the supporting (driver) question is the narrower one ... For judging, the supported (root)\nquestion reads the supporting questions' conclusions ... The fix separates the two uses of the edge\"), which speaks of seed\nedges; PRD 06 (\"B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion\"; \"A statement about A is A's evidence ... B learns it through A's\nconclusion\").\n\n### 2.1 What the rule changes in a decision's reading, and what it costs\n\nD265 says: \"a statement linked to a question and to a question that question depends on (the ledger's `dependencies`, the seeds'\nroot-and-driver pairs, transitively) keeps the narrower one only\". The rule keeps this for `supports` edges and **does not apply\nit to `reads` edges**. That is a proposed interpretation, not something D368 settles: D368 separates the two uses for seed edges\nand does not say that a dependency chosen by the matching call has no filing meaning. The council may take the interpretation\nunder D439; it is recorded as one.\n\nIts cost, measured on the one real case: with the `reads` edge \"data-center revenue reads total revenue\", the seven statements the\nreading step linked to both questions keep both homes, as they do today without any edge. Four of them belong under total revenue\n(the company-wide outlook; three sentences holding its own reported figure); three are about the segment's competition and D207's\nnarrowest home would be data-center revenue alone. So the interpretation preserves the four and leaves the three filed twice.\nThe literal reading of D265 does the opposite: it moves the three rightly and strips the four wrongly, one of them a statement\na total-revenue judgement cites.\n\n### 2.2 Duplicates and circles, on the reading relation\n| D368 (2026-11-05) | item 2, Alex: \"做\" (the matching step chooses a new question's dependencies; a later item may add one); item 4, settled by Claude on his delegation (\"好你来定\"): the two sentences quoted in section 2 | item 4's direction is kept as recorded, not measured (D536); this design is its \"fix separates the two uses\", extended to chosen edges (D538) |\n| D354, D370, D427 | judging, matching and the reading prompt are frozen as they run; preset questions are retired from the default | nothing here changes a frozen piece; a build is a new version the council or Alex opens |\n| The independent review of 2026-11-03 (`review-core-codex.md`, point 6) | six contracts a fix must settle: filing's use, cycles across kinds, article-opened questions' edges keeping their meaning, the direction label, citations, the 600-character cut | the first, second, third and fifth are answered by the rule and section 8; the fourth and sixth are open (section 7) |\n\n## 7. What this design leaves open (for the council; nothing here is assumed)\n\n1. **The representation**: a kind on the row, or two tables. The proof derives the kind from the writer's marks for stored\n   edges; a build would store it where the edge is written.\n2. **The direction label of a `supports` edge when the root reads the driver: a blocker for any seeded build.** Seed rows carry\n   `last_end`, outside the matching vocabulary (`first_is_bullish`, `last_is_bullish`, `same`, `opposite`); the request prints\n   the label as stored, and what it tells the new reader has not been established.\n3. **What the line carries**: the conclusion's first 600 characters and its band, not the implied outcome; a root might get a\n   band without the calculation behind it.\n4. **A theme named by a `reads` edge** would be shown twice (the theme block and the line). The census found no such pair\n   that both readers grant.\n5. **The strongest relations of the fresh week are not reachable at creation** (the Rubin shipping questions against the\n   revenue questions opened before them). Two decided or possible routes, neither built: the later addition (D191 point 2), or\n   letting a new question declare that it supports an existing one, which is D208's sentence for seeds and beyond D51's words\n   for article-opened questions. A choice, not assumed here.\n6. **The known cost of the narrowest rule under a `supports` edge** (the root's own figure in a sentence that also holds the\n   driver's) is unchanged by this design.\n7. **The selection itself**: which call is asked, what it is shown (the matching engine already stores a closed relation for\n   every compared pair, \"part of\" among them), its prompt audit and its measurement. A separate design.\n8. **D368 item 4's direction** stays recorded and unmeasured; a measurement needs a seeded configuration the default no longer\n   runs (D536).\n9. **Cells of table 1 no proof exercises**, which a build-ready contract must fill: a `supports` edge whose driver closes, with or\n   without a successor (a stored kind affects resolution); the later addition (D191 point 2); a stored ledger with `reads` rows\n   read through today's pages (the 395 rows are read here only by their marks).\n10. **The one home for the cases of 2.5**: the reading step's, the filing phase's subject (PRD 10); no rule for code is proposed.\n11. **Where decisions and code part, or decisions are silent** (2.6): a cycling edge is dropped with a note where PRD 06 says\n    refuse and ask for a correction; an awaited edge that would close a circle is left unbound without a record; \"historical\n    only\" has a reader and no writer; no decision says what happens to a later proposal with another direction.\n12. **The seed edge's label `last_end`**: undefined anywhere; it blocks both a seeded build (item 2) and the comparison of a\n    proposed edge with a `supports` edge in the same reading direction (2.2). Returned to the council; its meaning is not\n    invented here.\n\n95:    def retire(self, call_id):\n116:def build_request(setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, provider):\n251:def call(archive, cfg, setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, purpose, *, transport=None):\n11:- **The ruler is frozen** (§7.1; D261, D262): current measurements use it as it is, and no file of it changes. D372 combines the existing gold's core and supporting counts without changing the gold; optional entries remain neutral. Read §7.1 before any gold, score, judge, prompt or acceptance work.\n71:   frozen ruler (§7.1), never by a new ruler (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"我们是聊过这个事情的你难道没看文档吗\").\n175:   (§7.1). Never a full rerun per fix.\n197:  step's standard (§7.1) or a decision; a sentence with no such line is a proposal for Alex, not an edit. A reject sentence must not\n239:### 7.1 The ruler\n263:  2026-07-14 against reader B's ranges (a numeric question agrees when the gold range overlaps the chosen rung's band, a yes/no question\n273:- **The ruler is Alex's, never your own.** Gold, unit and the judge's reading are checked against what the product uses and fixed with\n299:- **The ruler counts only what it was built to count.** Before a quality claim, a reader who did not write the change also reads the\n402:- The extraction standard (§7.1, piece 1) is the step's standard. Its two sources are applied before designing a prompt, evaluation or\n487:0. No file of the frozen ruler (§7.1) has changed: `python3 -m pytest tests/test_frozen_rulers.py` is green.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
